    "Cin_is_measured_or_extracted": false,
    "false_unity_frequency_Hz": 8893952.702194152,
    "magnitude_at_1GHz_dB": 41.018103688950816,
    "explanation": "A rising unity crossing can exist with no active return loop; this proves the observed ratio alone is not a generally valid stability measure. It does not uniquely decompose the actual transistor network."
  },
  "findings": [
    "The previous helper only enumerated descending0dB crossings. All final curves also have ascending crossings near8.8-10.5MHz.",
    "At1GHz the ratio is about40dB at approximately+87degrees, consistent with a capacitive loading term. Full frequency data remain retained.",
    "The first descending-crossing180+phase values are arithmetic indicators under an unverified impedance/unilateral approximation, not independently established phase margins.",
    "This audit does not claim high-frequency oscillation; actual return ratio and closed-loop poles are not determined by these two saved voltages."
  ],
  "next_validation": [
    "Use a validated two-injection/two-port return-ratio method that retains loading and reverse transmission, or appropriate device-based gain nulling.",
    "Check applicability to all relevant nested loops and DC/load states; retain all crossings and complex response.",
    "Continue separate large-signal startup, line/load disturbance and long-pattern transient tests; finite transients alone do not prove unconditional stability."
  ],
